# Copy to .env and fill in. Nothing in this repository auto-loads a .env: export
# it yourself, or hand these to the server through your MCP client's `env` block
# or `docker run --env-file`.
#
# The three credential variables at the bottom are what you need to start.
# TASTYTRADE_ENV picks the endpoint. The optional knobs —
# MAX_ORDER_NOTIONAL_USD, TASTYTRADE_READ_ONLY, TASTYTRADE_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS,
# TASTYTRADE_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES, TASTYTRADE_HTTP_WALL_CLOCK_MS,
# TASTYTRADE_ACCEPT_VERSION, TASTYTRADE_USER_AGENT,
# TASTYTRADE_ALLOW_UNKNOWN_API_HOST, TASTYTRADE_CREDENTIAL_CHANNEL,
# TASTYTRADE_ALLOW_PROXY — are documented in the README.
#
# ---- WHICH ENDPOINT, AND WHAT IT COSTS YOU IF YOU GET IT WRONG -------------
#
# THE DEFAULT IS PRODUCTION. With TASTYTRADE_ENV unset and no
# TASTYTRADE_API_URL, this server talks to https://api.tastyworks.com: real
# accounts, real funds, and orders that cannot be undone. That default is
# deliberate — the sandbox serves no market data, so a server pointed there
# cannot quote, and a default that cannot do the job is not a safe one.
#
# TASTYTRADE_ENV takes one word, case-insensitive, surrounding whitespace
# tolerated:
#   production | prod | live      -> https://api.tastyworks.com
#   sandbox | cert | staging | sbx -> https://api.cert.tastyworks.com
#
# A value the server CANNOT read — `sandbx`, `prodution` — resolves to the
# SANDBOX and prints a banner on stderr saying so. Unset is a default; a typo is
# a failed instruction, and the two must not land in the same place.
#
# The line below names PRODUCTION, matching the default and matching the
# paste-ready blocks in README.md and server.json. It is written out rather than
# left off on purpose: a file that ships one environment while the documentation
# promises another sends whoever copies it somewhere they did not choose, and
# they find out from a fill.
#
# TO USE THE SANDBOX, change it to `sandbox`. Know what you are trading away:
# the sandbox currently answers every market-data and market-metrics route with
# HTTP 502, so quotes and market metrics do not work there. Instruments, option
# chains, futures, accounts, balances, positions, orders and the dry-run/order
# paths all do.
#
# Whichever you pick, TASTYTRADE_READ_ONLY=1 withholds and refuses every write
# and destructive tool while you look around. On production the server also
# prints an unmissable stderr banner at startup and tells the connected agent
# the same thing in the `instructions` it returns on initialize.
TASTYTRADE_ENV=production

# ---- TASTYTRADE_API_URL: only for a host that is not tastytrade's own ------
#
# An explicit base URL. It WINS over TASTYTRADE_ENV, so leave it unset unless
# you mean it. It exists for a gateway, a proxy or a local test double, and it
# is the only way to reach a host that is not one of tastytrade's.
#
# TASTYTRADE_ALLOW_UNKNOWN_API_HOST is the one to know about before you need
# it: if TASTYTRADE_API_URL names a host that is not a tastytrade API host, the
# server REFUSES TO START rather than send the refresh token and client secret
# there. Name that exact hostname in TASTYTRADE_ALLOW_UNKNOWN_API_HOST to allow
# it.
#
# TASTYTRADE_API_URL=https://your-gateway.example

# ---- The credential channel ------------------------------------------------
#
# If your machine routes outbound HTTP through a proxy, or trusts an extra
# certificate authority, this server says so on stderr and keeps going. Set
# TASTYTRADE_CREDENTIAL_CHANNEL=strict to make that a refusal instead, and name
# your own gateway's hostname in TASTYTRADE_ALLOW_PROXY to permit it.

# ---- Credentials — placeholders only. Never commit a real value. ----------
TASTYTRADE_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id
TASTYTRADE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret
TASTYTRADE_REFRESH_TOKEN=your_refresh_token
